</head>
<body class="min-h-screen text-slate-100 flex flex-col justify-between selection:bg-cyan-500 selection:text-slate-950">

  <!-- TOP HEADER -->
  <header class="border-b border-slate-800/80 bg-slate-950/90 sticky top-0 z-50 backdrop-blur-md">
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 h-16 flex items-center justify-between">
      <div class="flex items-center space-x-3">
        <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-gradient-to-tr from-cyan-600 to-amber-500 flex items-center justify-center">
          <i class="fa-solid fa-flask text-slate-950 text-xl font-bold"></i>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h1 class="font-extrabold text-lg bg-gradient-to-r from-white to-slate-400 bg-clip-text text-transparent">Inversion Science</h1>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-400">Meteorology & Algorithms</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <!-- Navigation Links -->
      <nav class="flex space-x-4 text-sm font-semibold">
        <a href="index.html" class="text-slate-400 hover:text-cyan-400 transition"><i class="fa-solid fa-chart-line mr-1"></i>Dashboard</a>
        <a href="guide.html" class="text-slate-400 hover:text-cyan-400 transition"><i class="fa-solid fa-book-open mr-1"></i>Guide</a>
        <a href="science.html" class="text-cyan-400"><i class="fa-solid fa-flask mr-1"></i>Science</a>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-8 space-y-8 flex-grow">
    
    <section class="glass-card rounded-3xl p-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-white mb-4 border-b border-slate-700 pb-2">What is a Temperature Inversion?</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Normally, as you climb higher in elevation, the air temperature drops (this is known as the normal lapse rate). A <strong>temperature inversion</strong> occurs when this rule is flipped upside down. A layer of warm air slides over the top of the mountains, acting like a lid. This lid traps cold, moist air down in the valley floor. 
      </p>
      <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        When the temperature of that trapped cold air reaches its dew point, it condenses into a thick deck of fog. If you hike up the mountain, you actually walk from the freezing fog <em>into</em> the warm, sunny air layer above.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="glass-card rounded-3xl p-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-white mb-4 border-b border-slate-700 pb-2">How This App Predicts Inversions</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Because Boulder inversions are notoriously difficult to predict, this dashboard fetches hourly atmospheric sounding data from the <strong>Open-Meteo API</strong>[cite: 3] specifically tailored to Boulder's coordinates (Lat 40.0150, Lon -105.2705)[cite: 3].
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-cyan-400 mb-3">The 5 Core Algorithm Criteria</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-300 mb-4">The dashboard calculates a 0-100% Inversion Probability Score based on five strict variables[cite: 3]:</p>
      
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700 p-4 rounded-xl">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-white text-sm"><i class="fa-solid fa-cloud text-cyan-400 mr-2"></i>1. Low Cloud Cover (Max 35 pts)</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 mt-1">Evaluates the presence of a saturated airmass sitting directly on the valley floor[cite: 3].</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700 p-4 rounded-xl">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-white text-sm"><i class="fa-solid fa-sun text-amber-400 mr-2"></i>2. Upper Sky Clearance (Max 25 pts)</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 mt-1">Checks mid and high-level cloud cover. A low fog deck isn't visually stunning if the sky above it is also cloudy[cite: 3].</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700 p-4 rounded-xl">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-white text-sm"><i class="fa-solid fa-temperature-arrow-up text-rose-400 mr-2"></i>3. Thermal Cap (Max 25 pts)</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 mt-1">Compares the surface temperature to the 750hPa layer (~8,000 ft). If the air at 8,000 ft is warmer than the surface, a strong inversion lid exists[cite: 3].</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700 p-4 rounded-xl">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-white text-sm"><i class="fa-solid fa-droplet text-blue-400 mr-2"></i>4. Moisture Saturation (Max 10 pts)</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 mt-1">Measures how close the surface temperature is to the dew point, ensuring fog can actually form[cite: 3].</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-700 p-4 rounded-xl md:col-span-2">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-white text-sm"><i class="fa-solid fa-wind text-emerald-400 mr-2"></i>5. Surface Wind Flow (Max 5 pts / Heavy Penalty)</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 mt-1">Rewards light easterly (upslope) winds which push plains moisture into the mountains. Strongly penalizes (subtracts 20 points) for westerly downslope Chinook winds over 14mph, which instantly scour the moisture away[cite: 3].</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="glass-card rounded-3xl p-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-white mb-4 border-b border-slate-700 pb-2">Calculating Cloud Ceiling</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Once the probability score is generated, the tool attempts to calculate the height of the fog deck (Cloud Ceiling). 
        It does this by assessing the <strong>temperature delta</strong> (the difference between the surface temp and the temp at 8,100 feet).
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-slate-300 text-sm">
        <li><strong>Delta > 2.5°C:</strong> A very strong lid creates a low, compressed cloud deck (~6,700 ft)[cite: 3].</li>
        <li><strong>Delta > 0°C:</strong> A standard lid creates a mid-level deck (~7,500 ft)[cite: 3].</li>
        <li><strong>Delta < 0°C:</strong> A weak lid allows moisture to billow high into the mountains (~8,300 ft), socking in the peaks[cite: 3].</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </main>
</body>
